.TH CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_REMOVE 3 "2026-09-21" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_event_loop_remove \- deregister a selectable from an event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_event_loop_remove(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function deregisters reg. It is safe to call from inside a
callback for the registration that you remove. Self-removal on an
error is a common pattern. It is also safe to call from any other
thread, and at the same time as an in-flight dispatch for the same
registration.

No later callback for this registration ever runs after this call
returns. This covers a callback that the loop already collected and
that has not started yet. One such callback waits in the queue of a
dispatch worker thread with num_reactor_threads > 1. But this call
does not wait for a dispatch that is already in flight at the moment
of the call.

A dispatch that is still in flight can still use whatever arg points
to. A caller that must not free that memory needs handlers.on_removed;
see ccol_event_loop_add(3). It fires exactly one time, and
asynchronously. It fires only after no dispatch of this registration,
and no other ccol_event_loop call that resolves it, can still be in
progress. A caller that is sure that no dispatch can be in flight at
the moment of the removal may still free arg at once, with no
on_removed. Self-removal from inside the callback of that exact
registration is one such case. A registration that the loop never
dispatched is another.

This function does not close an fd, and it does not touch the lifetime
of a queue. It releases only the registration bookkeeping of the
ccol_event_loop. This mirrors the "the caller owns the fd" contract of
ccol_selectable_from_fd.

Remove every registration of an fd before you close that fd, as
libevent and libuv also require. Once the last of them is removed, the
loop has already stopped watching the fd when this call returns, and
the fd number is free. A new registration of the same fd, or of a new
descriptor that reuses the number, is independent of the removed one.
Nothing that the loop still does for the removed registration (a
dispatch in flight, or the later release of its bookkeeping) changes
how the new one is watched. A registration whose fd is closed first
still holds the number until it is removed. A ccol_event_loop_add(3) of
a descriptor that reuses the number meanwhile fails with
ccol_not_permitted for the direction that the stale registration holds,
and it can fail with ccol_unexpected_failure for the other direction.

For a queue selectable or a ccol_channel selectable, the loop finishes
its own bookkeeping for that queue before this call returns. It
unlinks the waiter of the registration from the queue, and it releases
the registration bookkeeping. A dispatch that is already in flight is
a separate matter. Its callback holds the selectable that the loop
gave it, and it reaches the queue through that selectable. The queue
needs no on_removed handshake for this, unlike arg above. A destroy of
the queue waits for any such callback to finish with it; see
ccol_circular_queue_destroy(3). The ordinary sequence of drain, remove
and destroy is therefore safe from any thread. That thread must not
itself run one of the callbacks of this registration.

It is safe to call this function more than one time on the same reg.
This holds for calls one after another, and for calls that truly run
at the same time on different threads. The reg handle also stays safe
to pass to a later ccol_event_loop_modify(),
ccol_event_loop_pause()/_resume(), ccol_event_loop_reg_generation() or
ccol_event_loop_remove() call after a removal. The library resolves
reg against the registration table of its own loop before it
dereferences anything. It therefore always finds a handle that is
stale, or that something already removed, and it never risks a
use-after-free.

The two repeat-call cases return different values. Take a call that
runs strictly after an earlier ccol_event_loop_remove() on the same
reg returned. It finds that reg_h no longer resolves at all, because
that earlier call already released the slot of reg before it returned.
It gets ccol_invalid_args. That is the same outcome that
ccol_event_loop_modify(), _pause(), _resume() and
ccol_event_loop_reg_generation() already give for a reg that something
removed. A call that truly races another ccol_event_loop_remove() on
the same reg is different. Its own resolve can still succeed, in a
narrow window where the other call has not released the slot of reg
yet. It then sees that reg is already marked as removed. It returns
ccol_success and does nothing.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
loop  ccol_event_loop the registration belongs to.
reg   Registration to remove.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success        The function removed the registration. It also
                    gives this when another call on the same reg, which
                    truly runs at the same time, removed it first. See
                    DESCRIPTION.
ccol_invalid_args   loop is CCOL_EVENT_LOOP_INVALID, or a handle that
                    is stale, or one that something already destroyed.
                    Or reg is CCOL_EVENT_REG_INVALID, or it fails to
                    resolve for another reason. One such reason is an
                    earlier ccol_event_loop_remove() call that already
                    returned and that already removed it.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call from any thread. This includes a call at the same time as
a dispatch for the same registration. It also includes a call from
inside the callback of that registration.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
void on_readable(ccol_event_loop loop, ccol_event_reg reg,
                 ccol_selectable *sel, void *arg) {
    conn_ctx_t *ctx = (conn_ctx_t *)arg;
    char buf[512];
    ssize_t n = read(sel->fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
    if (n <= 0) {
        ccol_event_loop_remove(loop, reg);  /* self-removal, safe */
        close(sel->fd);
        free(ctx);
        return;
    }
    /* ... */
}
.fi
